Post-Herpetic Neuralgia
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Dermatomal neuropathic pain >90 days post-rash, Severe mechanical allodynia, History of zoster eruption

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-036
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of intractable burning pain over the left thorax preventing sleep and deep breathing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented severe, unremitting, sharp left-sided chest pain causing significant respiratory splinting and distress. Urgent in-person assessment is mandated to confidently exclude acute cardiopulmonary emergencies (e.g., acute coronary syndrome or pulmonary embolism) and provide potent neuropathic analgesia before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My ribs feel like they are wrapped in barbed wire and set on fire. I can't even wear a shirt, and breathing deep makes me want to scream."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a severe acute exacerbation of persistent, severe burning and lancinating pain that has persisted for 110 days (>90 days) after the resolution of a herpes zoster rash. The pain is strictly confined to the exact dermatomal distribution of the preceding varicella-zoster virus reactivation (Left T5/T6). The patient reports profound mechanical allodynia causing extreme pain from light friction, such as bedsheets or clothing, alongside severe hyperalgesia resulting in an exaggerated response to mildly noxious stimuli. Intermittent paroxysms of electric shock-like sensations are superimposed on the continuous baseline pain. The patient complains of altered local temperature perception within the affected dermatome, stating the skin feels "icy and burning" simultaneously. Sleep disruption is severe, and the unrelenting nature of the pain is driving secondary depressive symptoms.
Functional Status: Fully independent but severely limited by severe cutaneous allodynia; pacing the room shirtless.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation leading to cognitive fatigue; high risk of developing secondary pneumonia due to continuous shallow breathing (splinting).

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm (shallow) | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, guarding the left side of the chest, taking small, rapid breaths.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the left T5/T6 dermatome reveals hypopigmentation and distinct scarring visible directly over the previously affected skin, wrapping from the spine to the sternum. There are no active vesicular lesions. Exquisite mechanical allodynia is elicited by lightly touching the area with a cotton wisp. Cutaneous hypoesthesia (numbness) to pinprick is present within the core of the painful dermatome despite the allodynia. Cardiopulmonary examination reveals clear lung fields bilaterally and normal heart sounds, completely lacking signs of friction rub, consolidation, or ischemia.
Environmental Observations: House is very warm; patient utilizing a fan directly on the chest to 'numb' the skin.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and Ibuprofen providing zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Herpetic Neuralgia - Status: Acute Severe Pain Flare
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is strictly dermatomal, electrical/burning in quality, highly provoked by light cutaneous touch, and lacks crushing central pressure or jaw/arm radiation.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal oxygen saturation, clear lungs, no tachycardia >100, and pain is explicitly triggered by superficial skin contact rather than solely by deep inspiration.

Intercostal Muscle Strain - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of severe allodynia, altered temperature perception, distinct scarring from a previous zoster eruption, and neuropathic rather than mechanical muscular pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering an excruciating exacerbation of post-herpetic neuralgia. The intense, left-sided thoracic pain prompted an after-hours call due to fears of a heart attack and severe respiratory splinting. Delaying assessment would subject the patient to agonizing, unremitting pain, profound insomnia, and a high risk of basal atelectasis due to continuous shallow breathing. Urgent clinical examination successfully and definitively ruled out life-threatening cardiopulmonary emergencies, allowing for the immediate initiation of targeted, membrane-stabilizing neuropathic analgesia and topical treatments, strictly fulfilling MBS criteria for an urgent, necessary intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg orally STAT, followed by 75mg at night. Prescribed topical 5% Lidocaine patches to be applied strictly to the intact, painful skin for 12 hours on, 12 hours off.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Step 1: By applying the Lidocaine patch directly over the painful scarring, the patient actively numbs the hyperactive nerve endings locally, instantly allowing them to tolerate a shirt and take deep breaths.

Step 2: By taking ten deep, slow, and deliberate breaths every hour, the patient fully expands their lung bases, completely preventing the development of a secondary chest infection caused by shallow pain-breathing.

Step 3: By taking the Pregabalin precisely two hours before bed, the patient stabilizes the hyperactive central nerves, guaranteeing periods of restorative sleep free from the burning shocks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to titrate Pregabalin and monitor pain control.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department if the pain suddenly changes character to a deep, crushing chest pressure, radiates to the left arm or jaw, or if you become severely short of breath while sitting still.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Post-herpetic neuralgia can be a chronic, refractory condition; complete pain resolution takes time and medication titration.
Clinical Course: The topical patches provide immediate, localized relief. The oral nerve medication will gradually build up in your system over the next 1-2 weeks to calm the damaged nerve root and improve sleep.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for review if the pain completely fails to improve after 48 hours on the new medications, or if the skin breaks down or develops a new rash under the patches.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The old shingles virus permanently damaged the sensory nerve in your chest wall. Even though the rash is gone, the nerve is continuously misfiring, sending false, severe pain signals to your brain when anything touches the skin. Standard painkillers do not work. The patch numbs the skin, and the oral medication calms the misfiring nerve.
Actionable Steps: Apply the Lidocaine patch now. Practice deep breathing exercises hourly. Take the Pregabalin tonight.

